    "['pseudowire-class [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-class 1to3 encapsulation-type mpls', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-class 1to3 encapsulation-type mpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `pseudowire-class` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols l2circuit pseudowire-class 1to3 encapsulation-type mpls\\n##\\n\\nThis creates a pseudowire class named \"1to3\" with MPLS encapsulation, which is the Juniper equivalent functionality to the Cisco command. The Juniper command sets this under the L2circuit protocol configuration context.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-class 1to3 encapsulation-type mpls'}]]": 1,
    "['encapsulation [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'encapsulation-type mpls;', 'parent_command': ['set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-class 1to3 encapsulation-type mpls'], 'root': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-class 1to3 encapsulation-type mpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nencapsulation-type mpls;\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper devices, the equivalent command to Cisco's `encapsulation mpls` in a pseudowire context is `encapsulation-type mpls;`. This sets the pseudowire encapsulation to MPLS, which is the same functionality as the Cisco command.\", 'trans_template': 'encapsulation-type mpls;'}]]": 1,
    "['controller E1 [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, E1 controllers are configured under `interfaces` hierarchy\\n- The interface naming follows a similar pattern but uses hyphens instead of slashes (e1-1/0/1 vs E1 1/0/1)\\n- Juniper doesn't use the `controller` command structure that Cisco does - physical interfaces are configured directly under `interfaces`\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1'}]]": 1,
    "['framing [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-fpc/pic/port framing crc4', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/1'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `framing crc4` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces e1-fpc/pic/port framing crc4\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the E1 controller configuration is typically applied under the E1 interface hierarchy, and the exact interface naming depends on the chassis location (FPC/PIC/port numbers would replace the Cisco-style slot/subslot/port notation).\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-fpc/pic/port framing crc4'}]]": 1,
    "['channel-group [parameter1] timeslots [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/1'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/1 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31\\n##\\n\\nThis command:\\n1. Uses Juniper's interface naming convention (`e1-1/0/1` instead of `E1 1/0/1`)\\n2. Maintains the same channel group number (1)\\n3. Preserves the same timeslot range (1-31)\\n4. Uses Juniper's configuration syntax (`set` command structure)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31'}]]": 1,
    "['controller E1 [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"controller E1\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/2 channelized timeslots 1-31\\n##\\n\\nThe translation:\\n1. Juniper uses \"e1\" instead of \"E1\" for E1 interfaces\\n2. The interface naming follows a similar format (1/0/2)\\n3. The framing and channel-group commands are separated into two statements\\n4. Juniper uses \"channelized timeslots\" instead of \"channel-group x timeslots\"', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 channelized timeslots 1-31', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 channelized timeslots 1-31', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"controller E1\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/2 channelized timeslots 1-31\\n##\\n\\nThe translation:\\n1. Juniper uses \"e1\" instead of \"E1\" for E1 interfaces\\n2. The interface naming follows a similar format (1/0/2)\\n3. The framing and channel-group commands are separated into two statements\\n4. Juniper uses \"channelized timeslots\" instead of \"channel-group x timeslots\"', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 channelized timeslots 1-31'}]]": 1,
    "['framing [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'framing crc4;', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nframing crc4;\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent command for E1 controller configuration would be `framing crc4;` under the respective interface hierarchy. The semicolon is required in Juniper syntax. However, please be aware that Juniper's exact implementation might differ in context since their controller configuration structure isn't identical to Cisco's. The above is the direct translation of the framing parameter.\", 'trans_template': 'framing crc4;'}]]": 1,
    "['channel-group [parameter1] timeslots [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/1 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/2 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The Juniper syntax assumes the E1 controllers are represented as interfaces (e1-fpc/pic/port)\\n2. Both controller configurations are translated since both use identical channel-group settings\\n3. In Juniper, this would typically be configured under the `[edit interfaces]` hierarchy level\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/1 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4'], 'root': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 framing crc4', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/1 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31\\nset interfaces e1-1/0/2 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The Juniper syntax assumes the E1 controllers are represented as interfaces (e1-fpc/pic/port)\\n2. Both controller configurations are translated since both use identical channel-group settings\\n3. In Juniper, this would typically be configured under the `[edit interfaces]` hierarchy level\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces e1-1/0/2 channel-group 1 timeslots 1-31'}]]": 1,
