    "['no keepalive', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/1:1 no-keepalives', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"no keepalive\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/1:1 no-keepalives\\nset interfaces se-1/0/2:1 no-keepalives\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses \"no-keepalives\" instead of Cisco\\'s \"no keepalive\"\\n2. In Juniper, the interface naming for serial interfaces typically uses \"se-\" prefix\\n3. The command must be applied to each interface separately in Juniper (unlike Cisco where it\\'s configured under each interface)\\n4. The interface names (se-1/0/1:1 and se-1/0/2:1) should be adjusted to match your actual Juniper interface naming scheme', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/1:1 no-keepalives'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces se-1/0/2:1 no-keepalives', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"no keepalive\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces se-1/0/1:1 no-keepalives\\nset interfaces se-1/0/2:1 no-keepalives\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses \"no-keepalives\" instead of Cisco\\'s \"no keepalive\"\\n2. In Juniper, the interface naming for serial interfaces typically uses \"se-\" prefix\\n3. The command must be applied to each interface separately in Juniper (unlike Cisco where it\\'s configured under each interface)\\n4. The interface names (se-1/0/1:1 and se-1/0/2:1) should be adjusted to match your actual Juniper interface naming scheme', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces se-1/0/2:1 no-keepalives'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is actually to NOT disable the interface (since interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper). However, if you specifically need the exact opposite translation (to enable an interface), it would be:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nBut the most accurate translation of \"no shutdown\" (meaning to enable the interface) in Juniper would be the second option, as interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper OS and don\\'t require an explicit enable command.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is actually to NOT disable the interface (since interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper). However, if you specifically need the exact opposite translation (to enable an interface), it would be:\\n##\\ndelete interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nBut the most accurate translation of \"no shutdown\" (meaning to enable the interface) in Juniper would be the second option, as interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper OS and don\\'t require an explicit enable command.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces gigabit-etherne 2/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
